      modelAnswer: 'In chemistry a pure substance is a single element or compound that is not mixed with anything else. A pure substance has a specific, sharp melting point and boiling point at a given pressure, because all of its particles are the same and the forces between them are overcome at the same temperature. A mixture, or an impure sample, melts and boils over a range of temperatures, and its melting point is usually lower than that of the pure substance, because the impurities disrupt the regular arrangement of particles. So if a sample melts sharply at the known melting point it is likely to be pure, but if it melts over a range below this value it contains impurities. For example, pure water boils at exactly 100 °C, but salt water boils above 100 °C over a range of temperatures.'
