      modelAnswer: 'Iron rusts when it reacts with both oxygen and water to form hydrated iron(III) oxide; this is an oxidation reaction. Barrier methods such as painting, oiling, greasing or coating with plastic work by keeping oxygen and water away from the surface of the iron. They are cheap and suitable for objects such as bridges, car bodies and moving parts, but if the coating is scratched the iron underneath is exposed and starts to rust. Sacrificial protection uses a more reactive metal, such as zinc or magnesium, in contact with the iron. The more reactive metal loses electrons more readily, so it is oxidised instead of the iron, and it corrodes while the iron is protected. Magnesium blocks are attached to ship hulls and underground pipes in this way, and must be replaced as they are used up. Galvanising combines both methods: the iron is coated with zinc, which acts as a barrier, and if the coating is scratched the zinc still corrodes in preference to the iron. Electroplating with a less reactive metal such as tin or chromium gives an attractive barrier, but if it is damaged the iron can corrode even faster. The choice of method therefore depends on cost, the environment and whether the coating is likely to be damaged.'
